canvasplayer
------------

Graffiti Markup Language (GML) playback in the browser, on HTML5 <canvas>,
with no dependencies.

Online demo: https://jamiew.github.io/canvasplayer
Code: https://github.com/jamiew/canvasplayer

GML data comes from #000000book, an open database of GML files:
https://000000book.com/data/147.json


Running it locally
------------------

No build step, so any static server works:

    python3 -m http.server 8420

Open http://localhost:8420/?id=161. Also ?latest and ?random. Tags come from
000000book.com over JSONP, so you need the network but not a server. Switching
tags in the page keeps whatever mode, effects and layers you picked.

To debug a tag, turn on the DATA switches: "points" numbers the strokes and
marks every sample, "vectors" draws per-sample velocity, "bounds" shows the
capture screen against the drawing, "graph" plots speed. A GML file is usually
messier than it looks.

    node test.js


Using it
--------

    <div class="stage"><canvas id="canvas"></canvas></div>
    <script src="gml-player.js"></script>
    <script src="gml-source.js"></script>
    <script>
      GmlSource.load(147, function (tag) {
        new GmlPlayer(document.getElementById('canvas'), tag).play();
      });
    </script>

The canvas sizes itself to its parent, so give that element the dimensions you
want. gml-player.js has no dependencies and does not care where its data came
from -- feed it the same shape from anywhere:

    { id, app, rotate, strokes: [ {points: [[x, y, time], ...]} ] }

gml-source.js is only the #000000book adapter: it fetches a tag and flattens
the GML tree into that. It loads over JSONP, because the API sends no CORS
headers yet.


What it does
------------

Plays a tag in real time from the recorded timestamps, repairing the dead time
between strokes and saying so. Width follows the speed of the hand and tapers
like a marker. Ink pools where the pen dwelled, and runs from there.

Four modes. marker and chisel are handstyles: marker runs a spline through the
samples to take the staircase off a slow hand, and widens as the hand slows.
chisel sweeps a flat nib at a fixed angle, so width comes from direction rather
than speed. hairline and skeleton are the diagrammatic ones.

Five effects: ghost, pressure, bleed, jitter, fade. pressure drops the alpha
where the hand moved fast, bleed lays a wider faint pass under the stroke.

It opens in marker, with ghost and the drips on.

Runs of ink come from ranking each stroke's own samples slowest-first and
taking a few of them, spaced apart. A fixed speed threshold does not survive
contact with the archive: tag #158 never dropped under it and so never dripped
at all, while a slow tag dripped from every sample.

Paint runs when the wet film is thicker than the wall can hold, and the more of
it there is, the further it gets. So one pool feeds both: how slowly the pen was
moving and how long it sat there set how fat a run is and how far it falls, plus
a stable wobble. Runs fall straight down. They may lean as they go, but only by
a fraction of how far they have already fallen, because gravity is down.

Effects reach the runs as well as the stroke, since a run is ink too: fade ages
them, pressure lightens the fast ones, jitter moves them with the stroke they
hang off, bleed soaks them outwards.

Playback is monochrome in both light and dark, so the overlays separate
themselves by weight, dashes and alpha rather than colour.


